WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.794
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.794 --> 00:00:11.021
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; My parents,

00:00:11.021 --> 00:00:16.074
Tom and Judy met in college,
got married at age 23,

00:00:16.074 --> 00:00:19.460
and have been together ever since.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.170
So I guess I thought this
how the formula worked.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.430
You find someone young,
plug in that unknown variable as X and

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.585
out spits Y happily ever after.

00:00:32.585 --> 00:00:36.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So given that I just

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.200
turned 30 this month, I thought I'd
have it all figured out by now.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:47.450
I come home for the holidays and I have
one brother, Yates that's married with two

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:52.520
kids and another brother, Pierce
that's married with two kids and six

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.750
weddings scheduled for this year and I'm
feeling a small amount of panic mounting.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.580
Or claustrophobia, like walls of my
youth or uterus are closing in on me.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:11.150
Now this is no pity party,
I'm a smart succesful woman.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.200
I'm sophisticated yet
scrappy when I need to be.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.800
I'm well connected but
don't take myself too seriously.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.350
And I'm by no means trying
to sell myself here.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.390
The truth is I've been dating
ever since the fifth grade.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.955
Why haven't I figured this all out yet?

00:01:32.955 --> 00:01:37.225
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:37.225 --> 00:01:42.416
&gt;&gt; I asked the person who knows me best

00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:47.607
what my greatest weakness is, and

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:52.440
my mom replied you live in denial

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.559
that choices must be made.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.030
And she's right.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:05.200
But choosing a life partner,
it's a pretty daunting choice.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.380
I mean maybe Cinderella only
married Prince Charming

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:14.054
because she didn't meet Prince Eric first.

00:02:14.054 --> 00:02:17.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Look at that stud muffin.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.460
I decided to dive into the research, and

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.290
I stumbled upon the Stanford
marshmallow experiment.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.060
In this study,
if a kid can resist the smaller prize,

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.680
one marshmallow,
he'll get a bigger reward later.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:33.770
Two marshmallows.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.945
All I'm saying is, I'm holding out for
my two marshmallows.

00:02:38.945 --> 00:02:42.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Here's how I break down

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.970
my dating prospects.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.340
On the x axis,
I measure long term viability,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.550
which I call the penguin's axis,
because penguins mate for life.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.183
Is this guy good to bring
home to the parents?

00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:59.309
Total penguin,
on the y axis [LAUGH] on the y axis

00:02:59.309 --> 00:03:04.174
I measure how excited I am about someone,
so

00:03:04.174 --> 00:03:08.600
I call that the butterflies axis.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.000
Do I get nervous every time I send and
receive a text?

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.860
Definite butterflies.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.830
So, as previewed, I'm a teacher,
I want to fill this graph with examples.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:19.970
Like US Presidents.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:26.700
Bottom right quadrant you'd have someone
who is high penguin but low butterflies.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.398
Danger, I might be settling.

00:03:28.398 --> 00:03:31.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Jimmy Carter.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.453
Really nice guy but eh.

00:03:35.453 --> 00:03:41.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the top left quadrant,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.470
you have someone who's high butterflies,
but low penguin.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.065
Fun, but maybe not marriage material.

00:03:49.065 --> 00:03:54.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Bottom left quadrant.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.395
Not a penguin, and no butterflies well,
that's just wasting my time.

00:03:57.395 --> 00:04:00.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.330
I couldn't think of a US
president who's both.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.460
And I realized,
am I being unrealistic if I wait and

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.870
wait for this elusive top right
quadrant two marshmallow kind of man?

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.725
I needed some answers, so I set up focus
groups filled with my single friends.

00:04:20.725 --> 00:04:25.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And in these conversations,

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:28.710
I learned that Stanford grad students
think about this question a lot.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.440
Whereas I make graphs,
some of my friends make spreadsheets.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.380
One of my female friends Becca made
what she called a life partner matrix,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.822
where she breaks down what she is
looking for into three categories,

00:04:41.822 --> 00:04:45.721
emotional, intellectual and physical.

00:04:45.721 --> 00:04:48.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She further broke down each piece of

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.975
this pie into four slices.

00:04:50.975 --> 00:04:55.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For example, for

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.990
the emotional connection.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.150
Does he love my shortcomings?

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.040
Is he reciprocally vulnerable?

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.320
Do we argue fairly and lovingly?

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.220
In these focus groups,

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.410
my friends also shared just how difficult
their dating journeys have been.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:19.450
One of my gay friends, Angel shared how
he was making out with a guy at a club,

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:24.580
only to realize that guy, while kissing
him still had his eyes open and was

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.500
looking over Angel's shoulder, checking
out all the other guys in the room.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.220
Another friend, Marco, even made

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:40.470
a consulting slide deck analyzing his

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:45.401
lack of dating success on OkCupid.

00:05:45.401 --> 00:05:47.616
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So.

00:05:47.616 --> 00:05:53.004
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:53.004 --> 00:05:57.140
&gt;&gt; Maybe I have nerdy friends.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:02.060
Maybe, talking to Stanford grad
students doesn't accurately represent

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:02.815
the population.

00:06:02.815 --> 00:06:06.470
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I realized my research was flawed.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.470
If I wanted to figure out how
to choose a life partner,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.670
I need to talk to my friends
in committed relationships.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.920
So I sent them a survey and
asked one simple question.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:23.040
How did you choose your partner?

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.980
Here are three of my favorite responses.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.750
There was never a lightning
bolt moment per se,

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.370
just a gradual increase of connection and
affection.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:40.270
A couple of months in, I realized I
had something to lose if we broke up.

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:45.439
We met on a road trip,
got stuck in horrendous traffic.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.310
And I remember it as
the best drive of my life.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.070
I think I fell in love with him that day.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.620
I chose my partner by
following my heart and my gut.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.600
I thought about how to get
her when we were single.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.440
I thought about how to keep
her when we were dating.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.530
And now I think about how
to make her happy and

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.690
build a life together
now that we are married.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:10.870
Isn't that something?

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.580
In my research,
I talked to a guy named Nate Bagley

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.890
who traveled the country
interviewing married couples.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:24.299
His takeaway, don't settle for
mediocre love.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.400
Epic love exists and he's seen it.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.270
So, I reached out and I asked if I
could interview him over Skype and

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.345
in that conversation Nate Begley
he said to me, okay Jackie,

00:07:37.345 --> 00:07:40.480
you got five great guys in front of you.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:41.340
Who's the right choice?

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:47.440
So, I said, the one who
inspires me to be my best self.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.730
And he said, okay, they all do that.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:53.570
Who is the right choice?

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:59.060
So I said,
the one who makes me laugh the hardest.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.685
And he said, okay, they're all hilarious.

00:08:03.685 --> 00:08:05.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Who's the right choice?

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.290
So I said,
I come home from a business trip.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:16.525
Who's the first person I want to see and
he said okay, you want to see all of them.

00:08:16.525 --> 00:08:19.467
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Who's the right choice?

00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:25.344
And flustered I said, Nate, I don't
know how to choose where is this going?

00:08:25.344 --> 00:08:28.480
And he said to me, Jackie,

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:34.160
the right choice is the one you choose and
you make it right.

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.960
And if you're not in a place to
make that kind of commitment,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.910
you just might miss some
amazing candidates.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:46.690
In January,
I was dating a really nice guy.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:48.058
Coincidentally named Nathan.

00:08:48.058 --> 00:08:50.237
Total penguin.

00:08:50.237 --> 00:08:51.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.090
after a couple months I abandoned ship.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:57.660
I wasn't prepared to commit so I ended it.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:06.140
And talking to Nate Bagley I realized,
I'm the person at the club.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.660
Kissing with their eyes open.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.680
Constantly looking over someone's
shoulder for what's better.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:21.410
So he said to Nate Bagley, wait you
said don't settle for mediocre love.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.740
Epic love exist that's
what I'm waiting for.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:31.920
And he said, Jackie,
epic love isn't this innate thing,

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:38.350
epic love is the work you both put
in to make it better than yesterday.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:40.770
That's epic.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:44.130
Here I was.

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:49.740
So afraid of settling for
one marshmallow, and

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:55.280
I realized there's a difference
between settling and settling in.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:02.510
Settling in means loving someone for
their short-comings.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.790
Closing your eyes during the kiss.

00:10:05.975 --> 00:10:10.575
And working every day,
at being a better partner in this team.

00:10:12.548 --> 00:10:18.100
The takeaway here, is choose who
you love and love who you choose.

00:10:19.345 --> 00:10:22.290
Writing this talk has
changed everything for

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:26.950
me, because after all the research,
the focus groups, the interviews.

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:32.840
Turns out all the work had to be done
here, internally, not externally.

00:10:35.023 --> 00:10:40.602
The day after I broke up with Nathan,
the penguin, he came over,

00:10:40.602 --> 00:10:45.183
and he said to me,
I don't accept your resignation.

00:10:45.183 --> 00:10:48.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.930
I thought, whoa, that's pretty epic.

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:54.970
Is this penguin giving me butterflies?

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:02.227
So I withdrew my resignation.

00:11:02.227 --> 00:11:07.254
Now I don't know if Nathan
is my two marshmallow man,

00:11:07.254 --> 00:11:09.611
but I'm going to find out.

00:11:09.611 --> 00:11:13.883
Because this time, I'm going all in.

00:11:13.883 --> 00:11:14.998
Thank you.

00:11:14.998 --> 00:11:20.036
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:11:20.036 --> 00:11:24.659
[MUSIC]

